```

New:
```html
                        <input type="text" name="slug" x-model="slug" @input="slug = slugify(slug)" required minlength="3" maxlength="60"
                            pattern="[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*" title="Gunakan huruf kecil, angka, dan tanda hubung tanpa tanda hubung ganda atau di awal/akhir. Contoh: rangga-ayu" placeholder="rangga-ayu"
                            class="w-full border-0 bg-transparent px-3 py-2.5 text-sm outline-none">
                    </div>
                    <p class="mt-1.5 text-[10px] text-[#8a938b]">Huruf kecil, angka, dan tanda hubung. Tidak boleh sama dengan alamat yang sudah ada.</p>
                    <p x-show="slug.length > 0 && !slugValid" x-cloak class="mt-1 text-[11px] text-red-600">Alamat belum valid. Gunakan huruf kecil, angka, dan tanda hubung tanpa tanda hubung ganda atau di awal/akhir (contoh: rangga-ayu).</p>
                    @error('slug')<p class="mt-1 text-[11px] text-red-600">{{ $message }}</p>@enderror
```

And the script:
```js
function createInvitation() {
    return {
        selected: ...,
        submitting: false,
        slug: @json(old('slug', '')),
        slugify(value) {
            return String(value).trim().toLowerCase().replace(/\s+/g, '-').slice(0, 60);
        },
        get slugValid() {
            return /^[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$/.test(this.slug);
        },
        init() {
            this.$watch('slug', (value) => {
                const clean = this.slugify(value);
                if (clean !== value) this.slug = clean;
            });
        }
    };
}
```

Wait, there's an issue with `@json(old('slug', ''))` and the `$watch`: on init, `slug` is set from old value. If old value is invalid (e.g., after a failed submit with `rangga--ayu`), the `$watch` will slugify it: `slugify('rangga--ayu')` = `rangga--ayu` (unchanged). Good, it preserves the invalid value so the user can fix it and sees the error.

But if old value is `Andi Siti!`, slugify → `andi-siti!`. Hmm, preserves `!`. That's fine (shows the error).

Hmm, but actually, should the frontend strip `!`? The table says reject. Keep.

Now, there's a concern with the `@input` + `$watch` combo and the new `slugValid` getter: `slugValid` is a getter, Alpine handles it. Good.

Let me apply the edits.

Also, I should double-check: does the `pattern` attribute interfere with the `@error('slug')` display? When the browser blocks submit due to pattern, the server isn't hit, so no `@error`. Fine.

But wait — there's a potential conflict: if the user enters an invalid slug and the browser blocks with a native bubble, then the Laravel `@error` from a *previous* submission might still be displayed (if the page was re-rendered with old input). That's a minor edge case.

Hmm, actually there's a bigger consideration: should I even add `pattern`? If the browser's native validation blocks the form, the `@submit="submitting = true"` won't run, so the button stays enabled. Good.

But there's a subtle issue: native `pattern` validation message is shown only when the user submits. Some users might be confused by the English message. But I've added the inline `slugValid` hint which shows while typing, so they'll see the Indonesian hint. Good.

Let me apply.

Actually, let me reconsider one more time whether to make the frontend collapse double hyphens. The user's table says `rangga--ayu` → Ditolak. My implementation rejects it (pattern + backend). Good.

But hmm, thinking about real users: if someone types their names with a double space, `Rangga  Ayu`, the frontend `\s+`→`-` collapses it to `rangga-ayu`. Good.

OK let me apply the edits.

