```js
share() {
  const text = this.resolved;
  const usingNative = typeof navigator.share === 'function';
  this.record(usingNative ? 'web_share' : 'whatsapp');
  if (usingNative) {
    navigator.share({ text }).catch(() => {
      window.open('https://wa.me/?text=' + encodeURIComponent(text), '_blank', 'noopener');
    });
  } else {
    window.open('https://wa.me/?text=' + encodeURIComponent(text), '_blank', 'noopener');
  }
}
```
This calls `navigator.share` synchronously within the click handler (no await before it) — preserves user activation. Good. Record is fire-and-forget.

Wait: `this.record(...)` uses fetch which is async but doesn't await. Then `navigator.share` is called. The fetch starts but `navigator.share` is still called in the same task. Good.

Note: `navigator.share` on some browsers throws synchronously if not allowed; wrap in try/catch. Let me use:
```js
try { navigator.share({ text }); } catch (e) { window.open(fallback); }
```
But `navigator.share` returns a promise; sync throw is for missing user activation. The `.catch` handles async rejection. I'll do both: try/catch around the call, and `.catch` on the promise.

